      "worked": "The output of $g$ must lie in the domain of $f$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\operatorname{range}(g)\\cap\\operatorname{domain}(f)&=[0,\\infty)\\cap[-1,1]\\\\\\operatorname{range}(g)\\cap\\operatorname{domain}(f)&=[0,1].\\end{align*}$$\n\nThus only inputs satisfying $0\\le g(x)\\le1$ are allowed:\n\n$$\\operatorname{domain}(f\\circ g)=\\{x\\in\\mathbb{R}:0\\le g(x)\\le1\\}.$$\n\nSince the range of $g$ is $[0,\\infty)$, some real inputs produce outputs greater than $1$. These inputs are excluded, so the composite's domain is a proper subset of $\\mathbb{R}$. A specific interval for $x$ cannot be determined without more information about $g$.",
